{
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/master/Schemata/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Annex",
          "fullName": "Annex — AI Act conformity engine",
          "version": "0.1.0",
          "semanticVersion": "0.1.0",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/shi1720/LexHack",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art5.emotion-workplace",
              "name": "EuAiActArt5EmotionWorkplace",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No emotion inference in the workplace or education"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 5(1)(f) prohibits placing on the market, putting into service or using AI systems to infer emotions of a natural person in the areas of workplace and education institutions, except for medical or safety reasons."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 5(1)(f) prohibits placing on the market, putting into service or using AI systems to infer emotions of a natural person in the areas of workplace and education institutions, except for medical or safety reasons.\n\nRemove the emotion inference feature, or establish and document that the system is placed on the market for medical or safety reasons — the only carve-out in Article 5(1)(f). No amount of consent, disclosure or human review cures a prohibited practice.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 5(1)(f) — Prohibited AI practices — emotion inference at work or school: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/5/\nEU AI Act Art. 99(3) — Penalties — EUR 35 000 000 or 7 % of worldwide annual turnover: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/99/",
                "markdown": "**No emotion inference in the workplace or education**\nArticle 5(1)(f) prohibits placing on the market, putting into service or using AI systems to infer emotions of a natural person in the areas of workplace and education institutions, except for medical or safety reasons.\n**How to close it:** Remove the emotion inference feature, or establish and document that the system is placed on the market for medical or safety reasons — the only carve-out in Article 5(1)(f). No amount of consent, disclosure or human review cures a prohibited practice.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 5(1)(f) — Prohibited AI practices — emotion inference at work or school](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/5/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 99(3) — Penalties — EUR 35 000 000 or 7 % of worldwide annual turnover](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/99/)\n_In force from 2025-02-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "prohibition",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art4.ai-literacy",
              "name": "EuAiActArt4AiLiteracy",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Measures to support AI literacy"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 4 requires providers and deployers to take measures to support the development of AI literacy of their staff and other persons operating the system on their behalf. It binds every AI system, not only high-risk ones."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 4 requires providers and deployers to take measures to support the development of AI literacy of their staff and other persons operating the system on their behalf. It binds every AI system, not only high-risk ones.\n\nAdd a short internal page covering what the system does, what it cannot do, and how staff should treat its output. Article 4 as amended does not require you to guarantee any individual competence level — only to take measures.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 4 — AI literacy: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/4/",
                "markdown": "**Measures to support AI literacy**\nArticle 4 requires providers and deployers to take measures to support the development of AI literacy of their staff and other persons operating the system on their behalf. It binds every AI system, not only high-risk ones.\n**How to close it:** Add a short internal page covering what the system does, what it cannot do, and how staff should treat its output. Article 4 as amended does not require you to guarantee any individual competence level — only to take measures.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 4 — AI literacy](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/4/)\n_In force from 2025-02-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "quality-management",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art50.1.interaction-disclosure",
              "name": "EuAiActArt501InteractionDisclosure",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Tell people they are talking to an AI"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 50(1) requires providers of AI systems intended to interact directly with natural persons to design them so that those persons are informed they are interacting with an AI system, unless that is obvious to a reasonably well-informed, observant and circumspect person. Article 50(5) requires the information to be clear, distinguishable and given at the latest at the first interaction."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 50(1) requires providers of AI systems intended to interact directly with natural persons to design them so that those persons are informed they are interacting with an AI system, unless that is obvious to a reasonably well-informed, observant and circumspect person. Article 50(5) requires the information to be clear, distinguishable and given at the latest at the first interaction.\n\nShow a persistent, clearly distinguishable notice at the start of every conversation stating that the user is interacting with an AI system. In force since 2 August 2026; exposure is EUR 15 000 000 or 3 % of worldwide annual turnover.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 50(1) — Transparency obligations — interaction with natural persons: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/\nEU AI Act Art. 50(5) — Transparency obligations — manner and timing of disclosure: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/\nEU AI Act Art. 99(4)(g) — Penalties — EUR 15 000 000 or 3 % of worldwide annual turnover: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/99/",
                "markdown": "**Tell people they are talking to an AI**\nArticle 50(1) requires providers of AI systems intended to interact directly with natural persons to design them so that those persons are informed they are interacting with an AI system, unless that is obvious to a reasonably well-informed, observant and circumspect person. Article 50(5) requires the information to be clear, distinguishable and given at the latest at the first interaction.\n**How to close it:** Show a persistent, clearly distinguishable notice at the start of every conversation stating that the user is interacting with an AI system. In force since 2 August 2026; exposure is EUR 15 000 000 or 3 % of worldwide annual turnover.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 50(1) — Transparency obligations — interaction with natural persons](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 50(5) — Transparency obligations — manner and timing of disclosure](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 99(4)(g) — Penalties — EUR 15 000 000 or 3 % of worldwide annual turnover](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/99/)\n_In force from 2026-08-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "transparency",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art50.2.content-marking",
              "name": "EuAiActArt502ContentMarking",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Mark synthetic output in a machine-readable format"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 50(2) requires providers of AI systems generating synthetic audio, image, video or text to ensure outputs are marked in a machine-readable format and detectable as artificially generated or manipulated. Systems already on the market before 2 August 2026 have until 2 December 2026 under Article 111(4)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 50(2) requires providers of AI systems generating synthetic audio, image, video or text to ensure outputs are marked in a machine-readable format and detectable as artificially generated or manipulated. Systems already on the market before 2 August 2026 have until 2 December 2026 under Article 111(4).\n\nAttach a provenance manifest to every generated artefact (C2PA Content Credentials for image, audio and video). A visible \"AI-generated\" label is not enough: the statutory test is machine readability.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 50(2) — Transparency obligations — machine-readable marking of synthetic content: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/\nEU AI Act Art. 111(4) — Transitional provision — marking deadline of 2 December 2026: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/111/",
                "markdown": "**Mark synthetic output in a machine-readable format**\nArticle 50(2) requires providers of AI systems generating synthetic audio, image, video or text to ensure outputs are marked in a machine-readable format and detectable as artificially generated or manipulated. Systems already on the market before 2 August 2026 have until 2 December 2026 under Article 111(4).\n**How to close it:** Attach a provenance manifest to every generated artefact (C2PA Content Credentials for image, audio and video). A visible \"AI-generated\" label is not enough: the statutory test is machine readability.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 50(2) — Transparency obligations — machine-readable marking of synthetic content](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 111(4) — Transitional provision — marking deadline of 2 December 2026](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/111/)\n_In force from 2026-08-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "transparency",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art50.3.biometric-notification",
              "name": "EuAiActArt503BiometricNotification",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Notify people exposed to emotion recognition or biometric categorisation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 50(3) requires deployers of an emotion recognition system or a biometric categorisation system to inform the natural persons exposed to it of the operation of the system, and to process personal data in accordance with the GDPR."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 50(3) requires deployers of an emotion recognition system or a biometric categorisation system to inform the natural persons exposed to it of the operation of the system, and to process personal data in accordance with the GDPR.\n\nInform exposed persons of the operation of the system before they are exposed, and record the GDPR lawful basis for processing the biometric data.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 50(3) — Transparency obligations — emotion recognition and biometric categorisation: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/",
                "markdown": "**Notify people exposed to emotion recognition or biometric categorisation**\nArticle 50(3) requires deployers of an emotion recognition system or a biometric categorisation system to inform the natural persons exposed to it of the operation of the system, and to process personal data in accordance with the GDPR.\n**How to close it:** Inform exposed persons of the operation of the system before they are exposed, and record the GDPR lawful basis for processing the biometric data.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 50(3) — Transparency obligations — emotion recognition and biometric categorisation](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/)\n_In force from 2026-08-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "transparency",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art3.role-determination",
              "name": "EuAiActArt3RoleDetermination",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Determine and record whether you are the provider or the deployer"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 3(3) makes whoever develops an AI system and places it on the market or puts it into service under their own name or trademark the provider; Article 3(4) makes whoever uses one under their own authority the deployer. The distinction decides which Article 50 transparency duty binds you today — 50(1) and 50(2) fall on providers, 50(4) on deployers — and, from 2 December 2027, whether the Chapter III provider stack attaches at all."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 3(3) makes whoever develops an AI system and places it on the market or puts it into service under their own name or trademark the provider; Article 3(4) makes whoever uses one under their own authority the deployer. The distinction decides which Article 50 transparency duty binds you today — 50(1) and 50(2) fall on providers, 50(4) on deployers — and, from 2 December 2027, whether the Chapter III provider stack attaches at all.\n\nRecord the determination in writing, with the reasoning: who places the system on the market, under whose name, and whether it is supplied to third parties or used only internally. If you repoint a general-purpose AI system at an Annex III use case you may become the provider under Article 25(1)(c) even without developing it. Then check whether your model vendor has specified that its system is \"not to be changed into a high-risk AI system\": that switches off the Article 25(2) duty to hand you the documentation you would need for Annex IV.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 3(3) — Definitions — 'provider': https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/\nEU AI Act Art. 3(4) — Definitions — 'deployer': https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/\nEU AI Act Art. 50 — Transparency obligations — allocated by role: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/",
                "markdown": "**Determine and record whether you are the provider or the deployer**\nArticle 3(3) makes whoever develops an AI system and places it on the market or puts it into service under their own name or trademark the provider; Article 3(4) makes whoever uses one under their own authority the deployer. The distinction decides which Article 50 transparency duty binds you today — 50(1) and 50(2) fall on providers, 50(4) on deployers — and, from 2 December 2027, whether the Chapter III provider stack attaches at all.\n**How to close it:** Record the determination in writing, with the reasoning: who places the system on the market, under whose name, and whether it is supplied to third parties or used only internally. If you repoint a general-purpose AI system at an Annex III use case you may become the provider under Article 25(1)(c) even without developing it. Then check whether your model vendor has specified that its system is \"not to be changed into a high-risk AI system\": that switches off the Article 25(2) duty to hand you the documentation you would need for Annex IV.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 3(3) — Definitions — 'provider'](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 3(4) — Definitions — 'deployer'](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 50 — Transparency obligations — allocated by role](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/)\n_In force from 2026-08-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "documentation",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art9.risk-management",
              "name": "EuAiActArt9RiskManagement",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Risk management system across the lifecycle"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 9 requires a documented, continuous and iterative risk management system run throughout the lifecycle: identify and analyse risks under the intended purpose and reasonably foreseeable misuse, evaluate risks from post-market monitoring, adopt targeted measures, and judge residual risk acceptable."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 9 requires a documented, continuous and iterative risk management system run throughout the lifecycle: identify and analyse risks under the intended purpose and reasonably foreseeable misuse, evaluate risks from post-market monitoring, adopt targeted measures, and judge residual risk acceptable.\n\nEstablish a risk register covering risks under the intended purpose and under reasonably foreseeable misuse, the measures adopted, and the residual-risk acceptance.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 9 — Risk management system: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/9/\nEU AI Act Annex IV, point 5 — Technical documentation — risk management system: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/iv/",
                "markdown": "**Risk management system across the lifecycle**\nArticle 9 requires a documented, continuous and iterative risk management system run throughout the lifecycle: identify and analyse risks under the intended purpose and reasonably foreseeable misuse, evaluate risks from post-market monitoring, adopt targeted measures, and judge residual risk acceptable.\n**How to close it:** Establish a risk register covering risks under the intended purpose and under reasonably foreseeable misuse, the measures adopted, and the residual-risk acceptance.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 9 — Risk management system](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/9/)\n- [EU AI Act Annex IV, point 5 — Technical documentation — risk management system](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/iv/)\n_In force from 2027-12-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "risk-management",
                  "upcoming"
                ],
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art10.bias-examination",
              "name": "EuAiActArt10BiasExamination",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Examine training and evaluation data for bias"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 10(2)(f) requires examination in view of possible biases likely to affect health and safety, negatively impact fundamental rights, or lead to discrimination prohibited under Union law — especially where outputs influence inputs for future operations. Article 10(2)(g) requires measures to detect, prevent and mitigate them."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 10(2)(f) requires examination in view of possible biases likely to affect health and safety, negatively impact fundamental rights, or lead to discrimination prohibited under Union law — especially where outputs influence inputs for future operations. Article 10(2)(g) requires measures to detect, prevent and mitigate them.\n\nAdd a measurement that computes outcome rates per protected group and an impact ratio, run it on every release, and retain the results. Article 10(2)(f) cannot be satisfied by assertion.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 10(2)(f) — Data and data governance — examination for possible biases: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/10/\nEU AI Act Art. 10(2)(g) — Data and data governance — bias mitigation measures: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/10/",
                "markdown": "**Examine training and evaluation data for bias**\nArticle 10(2)(f) requires examination in view of possible biases likely to affect health and safety, negatively impact fundamental rights, or lead to discrimination prohibited under Union law — especially where outputs influence inputs for future operations. Article 10(2)(g) requires measures to detect, prevent and mitigate them.\n**How to close it:** Add a measurement that computes outcome rates per protected group and an impact ratio, run it on every release, and retain the results. Article 10(2)(f) cannot be satisfied by assertion.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 10(2)(f) — Data and data governance — examination for possible biases](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/10/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 10(2)(g) — Data and data governance — bias mitigation measures](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/10/)\n_In force from 2027-12-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "data-governance",
                  "upcoming"
                ],
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art10.data-governance",
              "name": "EuAiActArt10DataGovernance",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Document data provenance and preparation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 10(2)(b)-(e) requires documented data collection processes and the origin of the data — including, for personal data, the original purpose of collection — data preparation operations, the assumptions the data encodes, and an assessment of availability, quantity and suitability."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 10(2)(b)-(e) requires documented data collection processes and the origin of the data — including, for personal data, the original purpose of collection — data preparation operations, the assumptions the data encodes, and an assessment of availability, quantity and suitability.\n\nWrite a datasheet for each training, validation and testing dataset: where it came from, how it was selected and labelled, what it is assumed to represent, and what is missing from it.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 10(2) — Data and data governance — governance practices: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/10/\nEU AI Act Annex IV, point 2(d) — Technical documentation — datasheets: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/iv/",
                "markdown": "**Document data provenance and preparation**\nArticle 10(2)(b)-(e) requires documented data collection processes and the origin of the data — including, for personal data, the original purpose of collection — data preparation operations, the assumptions the data encodes, and an assessment of availability, quantity and suitability.\n**How to close it:** Write a datasheet for each training, validation and testing dataset: where it came from, how it was selected and labelled, what it is assumed to represent, and what is missing from it.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 10(2) — Data and data governance — governance practices](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/10/)\n- [EU AI Act Annex IV, point 2(d) — Technical documentation — datasheets](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/iv/)\n_In force from 2027-12-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "data-governance",
                  "upcoming"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art11.technical-documentation",
              "name": "EuAiActArt11TechnicalDocumentation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Annex IV technical documentation exists and is current"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 11(1) requires technical documentation to be drawn up before the system is placed on the market and kept up to date, containing at least the elements set out in Annex IV. SMEs, start-ups and small mid-caps may provide it in simplified form."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 11(1) requires technical documentation to be drawn up before the system is placed on the market and kept up to date, containing at least the elements set out in Annex IV. SMEs, start-ups and small mid-caps may provide it in simplified form.\n\nGenerate the dossier. Annex produces a complete Annex IV skeleton at docs/ai-act/annex-iv-technical-documentation.md, populated from the evidence in this scan, with every unanswerable point marked TODO rather than invented.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 11(1) — Technical documentation: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/11/\nEU AI Act Annex IV — Technical documentation referred to in Article 11(1): https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/iv/",
                "markdown": "**Annex IV technical documentation exists and is current**\nArticle 11(1) requires technical documentation to be drawn up before the system is placed on the market and kept up to date, containing at least the elements set out in Annex IV. SMEs, start-ups and small mid-caps may provide it in simplified form.\n**How to close it:** Generate the dossier. Annex produces a complete Annex IV skeleton at docs/ai-act/annex-iv-technical-documentation.md, populated from the evidence in this scan, with every unanswerable point marked TODO rather than invented.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 11(1) — Technical documentation](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/11/)\n- [EU AI Act Annex IV — Technical documentation referred to in Article 11(1)](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/iv/)\n_In force from 2027-12-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "documentation",
                  "upcoming"
                ],
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art12.record-keeping",
              "name": "EuAiActArt12RecordKeeping",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Automatic logging of events over the system lifetime"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 12(1) requires high-risk AI systems to technically allow the automatic recording of events over their lifetime. Article 12(2) requires logging that enables identification of situations that may result in an Article 79(1) risk or a substantial modification, and that facilitates post-market monitoring."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 12(1) requires high-risk AI systems to technically allow the automatic recording of events over their lifetime. Article 12(2) requires logging that enables identification of situations that may result in an Article 79(1) risk or a substantial modification, and that facilitates post-market monitoring.\n\nRecord every inference: a decision identifier, the model and prompt version, digests of input and output, the outcome, the confidence, and who reviewed it.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 12(1) — Record-keeping — automatic logging: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/12/\nEU AI Act Art. 12(2) — Record-keeping — what the logs must enable: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/12/\nEU AI Act Art. 21(2) — Cooperation — access to logs on reasoned request: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/21/",
                "markdown": "**Automatic logging of events over the system lifetime**\nArticle 12(1) requires high-risk AI systems to technically allow the automatic recording of events over their lifetime. Article 12(2) requires logging that enables identification of situations that may result in an Article 79(1) risk or a substantial modification, and that facilitates post-market monitoring.\n**How to close it:** Record every inference: a decision identifier, the model and prompt version, digests of input and output, the outcome, the confidence, and who reviewed it.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 12(1) — Record-keeping — automatic logging](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/12/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 12(2) — Record-keeping — what the logs must enable](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/12/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 21(2) — Cooperation — access to logs on reasoned request](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/21/)\n_In force from 2027-12-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "record-keeping",
                  "upcoming"
                ],
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art13.instructions-for-use",
              "name": "EuAiActArt13InstructionsForUse",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Instructions for use for deployers"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 13(2)-(3) requires high-risk systems to be accompanied by concise, complete, correct and clear instructions for use covering provider identity, capabilities and limitations, declared accuracy metrics, foreseeable misuse, explanation capabilities, subgroup performance, input specifications, pre-determined changes, human oversight measures, resource needs and how the deployer reads the logs."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 13(2)-(3) requires high-risk systems to be accompanied by concise, complete, correct and clear instructions for use covering provider identity, capabilities and limitations, declared accuracy metrics, foreseeable misuse, explanation capabilities, subgroup performance, input specifications, pre-determined changes, human oversight measures, resource needs and how the deployer reads the logs.\n\nWrite the instructions against Article 13(3)(a)-(f), including the seven romanettes of (b). The hardest and most important is (b)(v): accuracy for specific persons or groups, not just overall.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 13(3) — Transparency and provision of information to deployers: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/13/",
                "markdown": "**Instructions for use for deployers**\nArticle 13(2)-(3) requires high-risk systems to be accompanied by concise, complete, correct and clear instructions for use covering provider identity, capabilities and limitations, declared accuracy metrics, foreseeable misuse, explanation capabilities, subgroup performance, input specifications, pre-determined changes, human oversight measures, resource needs and how the deployer reads the logs.\n**How to close it:** Write the instructions against Article 13(3)(a)-(f), including the seven romanettes of (b). The hardest and most important is (b)(v): accuracy for specific persons or groups, not just overall.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 13(3) — Transparency and provision of information to deployers](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/13/)\n_In force from 2027-12-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "transparency",
                  "upcoming"
                ],
