            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art14.human-oversight",
              "name": "EuAiActArt14HumanOversight",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Effective human oversight while the system is in use"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 14 requires high-risk systems to be designed so they can be effectively overseen by natural persons, who must be able to understand capacities and limitations, remain aware of automation bias, correctly interpret output, decide not to use the system or disregard, override or reverse its output, and intervene or interrupt it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 14 requires high-risk systems to be designed so they can be effectively overseen by natural persons, who must be able to understand capacities and limitations, remain aware of automation bias, correctly interpret output, decide not to use the system or disregard, override or reverse its output, and intervene or interrupt it.\n\nImplement a human review step (Art. 14(4)(d)), an override path (Art. 14(4)(d)), a stop control (Art. 14(4)(e)). Article 14 is the requirement auditors probe first, because it is the one that decides whether a wrong output becomes a wrong decision.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 14(1) — Human oversight: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/\nEU AI Act Art. 14(4)(d) — Human oversight — disregard, override or reverse the output: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/\nEU AI Act Art. 14(4)(e) — Human oversight — 'stop' button or similar procedure: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/",
                "markdown": "**Effective human oversight while the system is in use**\nArticle 14 requires high-risk systems to be designed so they can be effectively overseen by natural persons, who must be able to understand capacities and limitations, remain aware of automation bias, correctly interpret output, decide not to use the system or disregard, override or reverse its output, and intervene or interrupt it.\n**How to close it:** Implement a human review step (Art. 14(4)(d)), an override path (Art. 14(4)(d)), a stop control (Art. 14(4)(e)). Article 14 is the requirement auditors probe first, because it is the one that decides whether a wrong output becomes a wrong decision.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 14(1) — Human oversight](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 14(4)(d) — Human oversight — disregard, override or reverse the output](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 14(4)(e) — Human oversight — 'stop' button or similar procedure](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/)\n_In force from 2027-12-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "human-oversight",
                  "upcoming"
                ],
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art15.accuracy",
              "name": "EuAiActArt15Accuracy",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Declared accuracy metrics measured against a fixed set"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 15(1) requires an appropriate level of accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity, consistent throughout the lifecycle. Article 15(3) requires the declared accuracy levels and the relevant accuracy metrics to be stated in the instructions for use."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 15(1) requires an appropriate level of accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity, consistent throughout the lifecycle. Article 15(3) requires the declared accuracy levels and the relevant accuracy metrics to be stated in the instructions for use.\n\nBuild a fixed evaluation set with a declared metric and a threshold, run it on every release, and publish the result in the instructions for use.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 15(1) — Accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/15/\nEU AI Act Art. 15(3) — Declared accuracy levels and metrics: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/15/",
                "markdown": "**Declared accuracy metrics measured against a fixed set**\nArticle 15(1) requires an appropriate level of accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity, consistent throughout the lifecycle. Article 15(3) requires the declared accuracy levels and the relevant accuracy metrics to be stated in the instructions for use.\n**How to close it:** Build a fixed evaluation set with a declared metric and a threshold, run it on every release, and publish the result in the instructions for use.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 15(1) — Accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/15/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 15(3) — Declared accuracy levels and metrics](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/15/)\n_In force from 2027-12-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "accuracy-robustness",
                  "upcoming"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art15.cybersecurity",
              "name": "EuAiActArt15Cybersecurity",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "AI-specific cybersecurity measures"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 15(5) requires high-risk systems to be resilient against attempts by unauthorised third parties to alter their use, outputs or performance, with measures where appropriate to prevent, detect, respond to, resolve and control data poisoning, model poisoning, adversarial examples, model evasion and confidentiality attacks."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 15(5) requires high-risk systems to be resilient against attempts by unauthorised third parties to alter their use, outputs or performance, with measures where appropriate to prevent, detect, respond to, resolve and control data poisoning, model poisoning, adversarial examples, model evasion and confidentiality attacks.\n\nAdd input and output moderation on the model path, and an adversarial test suite covering prompt injection, jailbreaks and model evasion.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 15(5) — Cybersecurity — AI-specific attack surface: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/15/",
                "markdown": "**AI-specific cybersecurity measures**\nArticle 15(5) requires high-risk systems to be resilient against attempts by unauthorised third parties to alter their use, outputs or performance, with measures where appropriate to prevent, detect, respond to, resolve and control data poisoning, model poisoning, adversarial examples, model evasion and confidentiality attacks.\n**How to close it:** Add input and output moderation on the model path, and an adversarial test suite covering prompt injection, jailbreaks and model evasion.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 15(5) — Cybersecurity — AI-specific attack surface](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/15/)\n_In force from 2027-12-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "accuracy-robustness",
                  "upcoming"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art17.quality-management",
              "name": "EuAiActArt17QualityManagement",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Quality management system"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 17 requires a documented quality management system covering, among thirteen aspects, the regulatory compliance strategy, design and development controls, examination and validation procedures and their frequency, data management, the risk management system, post-market monitoring, incident reporting procedures, record-keeping and an accountability framework. Implementation must be proportionate to the size of the organisation."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 17 requires a documented quality management system covering, among thirteen aspects, the regulatory compliance strategy, design and development controls, examination and validation procedures and their frequency, data management, the risk management system, post-market monitoring, incident reporting procedures, record-keeping and an accountability framework. Implementation must be proportionate to the size of the organisation.\n\nArticle 17(1) asks for the compliance strategy, validation procedures and their frequency, and an accountability framework to be written down — not only implemented in tooling.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 17(1) — Quality management system: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/17/\nEU AI Act Art. 17(2) — Proportionality for SMEs: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/17/",
                "markdown": "**Quality management system**\nArticle 17 requires a documented quality management system covering, among thirteen aspects, the regulatory compliance strategy, design and development controls, examination and validation procedures and their frequency, data management, the risk management system, post-market monitoring, incident reporting procedures, record-keeping and an accountability framework. Implementation must be proportionate to the size of the organisation.\n**How to close it:** Article 17(1) asks for the compliance strategy, validation procedures and their frequency, and an accountability framework to be written down — not only implemented in tooling.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 17(1) — Quality management system](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/17/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 17(2) — Proportionality for SMEs](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/17/)\n_In force from 2027-12-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "quality-management",
                  "upcoming"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art72.post-market-monitoring",
              "name": "EuAiActArt72PostMarketMonitoring",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Post-market monitoring plan"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 72 requires a documented post-market monitoring system, proportionate to the risks, that actively and systematically collects and analyses data on performance throughout the lifetime of the system. Article 72(3) makes the monitoring plan part of the Annex IV technical documentation."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 72 requires a documented post-market monitoring system, proportionate to the risks, that actively and systematically collects and analyses data on performance throughout the lifetime of the system. Article 72(3) makes the monitoring plan part of the Annex IV technical documentation.\n\nDefine the signals you will collect once the system is live, the thresholds that trigger action, and who owns each one.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 72(1) — Post-market monitoring by providers: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/72/\nEU AI Act Annex IV, point 9 — Technical documentation — post-market monitoring plan: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/iv/",
                "markdown": "**Post-market monitoring plan**\nArticle 72 requires a documented post-market monitoring system, proportionate to the risks, that actively and systematically collects and analyses data on performance throughout the lifetime of the system. Article 72(3) makes the monitoring plan part of the Annex IV technical documentation.\n**How to close it:** Define the signals you will collect once the system is live, the thresholds that trigger action, and who owns each one.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 72(1) — Post-market monitoring by providers](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/72/)\n- [EU AI Act Annex IV, point 9 — Technical documentation — post-market monitoring plan](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/iv/)\n_In force from 2026-08-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "post-market",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art73.incident-reporting",
              "name": "EuAiActArt73IncidentReporting",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Serious incident reporting procedure"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 73 requires providers to report serious incidents to the market surveillance authority: within 2 days for a widespread infringement or an Article 3(49)(b) incident, within 10 days where a person has died, and in any event within 15 days otherwise. Article 73(6) forbids altering the system in a way that affects the later evaluation of causes before informing the authorities."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 73 requires providers to report serious incidents to the market surveillance authority: within 2 days for a widespread infringement or an Article 3(49)(b) incident, within 10 days where a person has died, and in any event within 15 days otherwise. Article 73(6) forbids altering the system in a way that affects the later evaluation of causes before informing the authorities.\n\nWrite the procedure, name the authority, and start the clock at the moment of awareness rather than the moment of confirmation.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 73(2) — Reporting of serious incidents — 15-day default: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/73/\nEU AI Act Art. 73(3) — Reporting of serious incidents — 2 days: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/73/\nEU AI Act Art. 73(4) — Reporting of serious incidents — 10 days where a person has died: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/73/",
                "markdown": "**Serious incident reporting procedure**\nArticle 73 requires providers to report serious incidents to the market surveillance authority: within 2 days for a widespread infringement or an Article 3(49)(b) incident, within 10 days where a person has died, and in any event within 15 days otherwise. Article 73(6) forbids altering the system in a way that affects the later evaluation of causes before informing the authorities.\n**How to close it:** Write the procedure, name the authority, and start the clock at the moment of awareness rather than the moment of confirmation.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 73(2) — Reporting of serious incidents — 15-day default](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/73/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 73(3) — Reporting of serious incidents — 2 days](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/73/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 73(4) — Reporting of serious incidents — 10 days where a person has died](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/73/)\n_In force from 2026-08-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "incident-response",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art49.registration",
              "name": "EuAiActArt49Registration",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Registration in the EU database"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 49(1) requires the provider of an Annex III high-risk system (other than point 2) to register itself and the system in the EU database before placing it on the market. Article 49(2) requires the same registration even where the provider concludes under Article 6(3) that the system is not high-risk."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 49(1) requires the provider of an Annex III high-risk system (other than point 2) to register itself and the system in the EU database before placing it on the market. Article 49(2) requires the same registration even where the provider concludes under Article 6(3) that the system is not high-risk.\n\nRegister the system before placing it on the market. Note the trap in Article 49(2): concluding under Article 6(3) that your system is *not* high-risk does not exempt you — it still has to be registered, and the assessment has to be documented beforehand under Article 6(4).\n\nEU AI Act Art. 49(1) — Registration of high-risk AI systems: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/49/\nEU AI Act Art. 49(2) — Registration where the provider claims the Article 6(3) derogation: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/49/\nEU AI Act Art. 6(4) — Documented assessment where a provider considers a system not high-risk: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/6/",
                "markdown": "**Registration in the EU database**\nArticle 49(1) requires the provider of an Annex III high-risk system (other than point 2) to register itself and the system in the EU database before placing it on the market. Article 49(2) requires the same registration even where the provider concludes under Article 6(3) that the system is not high-risk.\n**How to close it:** Register the system before placing it on the market. Note the trap in Article 49(2): concluding under Article 6(3) that your system is *not* high-risk does not exempt you — it still has to be registered, and the assessment has to be documented beforehand under Article 6(4).\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 49(1) — Registration of high-risk AI systems](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/49/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 49(2) — Registration where the provider claims the Article 6(3) derogation](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/49/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 6(4) — Documented assessment where a provider considers a system not high-risk](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/6/)\n_In force from 2026-08-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "registration",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art26.deployer-obligations",
              "name": "EuAiActArt26DeployerObligations",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Deployer duties: assigned overseers, input data, and telling the workforce"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 26 puts duties on the deployer, not the provider: use the system in accordance with the instructions for use (26(1)), assign human oversight to natural persons who have the necessary competence, training and authority and the support to exercise it (26(2)), ensure that input data is relevant and sufficiently representative for the intended purpose so far as the deployer controls it (26(4)), monitor operation and inform the provider and the market surveillance authority where a risk under Article 79(1) arises (26(5)), keep the automatically generated logs for at least six months (26(6)), and — before putting a high-risk system into service at the workplace — inform workers' representatives and the affected workers that they will be subject to it (26(7))."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 26 puts duties on the deployer, not the provider: use the system in accordance with the instructions for use (26(1)), assign human oversight to natural persons who have the necessary competence, training and authority and the support to exercise it (26(2)), ensure that input data is relevant and sufficiently representative for the intended purpose so far as the deployer controls it (26(4)), monitor operation and inform the provider and the market surveillance authority where a risk under Article 79(1) arises (26(5)), keep the automatically generated logs for at least six months (26(6)), and — before putting a high-risk system into service at the workplace — inform workers' representatives and the affected workers that they will be subject to it (26(7)).\n\nArticle 26(7) is a duty on the employer and it is easy to miss because it sits outside the technical requirements: before a high-risk system is used at the workplace, the affected workers and their representatives have to be told. Record when, and to whom.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 26(2) — Obligations of deployers — assigned human oversight: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/26/\nEU AI Act Art. 26(4) — Obligations of deployers — input data: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/26/\nEU AI Act Art. 26(7) — Obligations of deployers — informing workers: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/26/",
                "markdown": "**Deployer duties: assigned overseers, input data, and telling the workforce**\nArticle 26 puts duties on the deployer, not the provider: use the system in accordance with the instructions for use (26(1)), assign human oversight to natural persons who have the necessary competence, training and authority and the support to exercise it (26(2)), ensure that input data is relevant and sufficiently representative for the intended purpose so far as the deployer controls it (26(4)), monitor operation and inform the provider and the market surveillance authority where a risk under Article 79(1) arises (26(5)), keep the automatically generated logs for at least six months (26(6)), and — before putting a high-risk system into service at the workplace — inform workers' representatives and the affected workers that they will be subject to it (26(7)).\n**How to close it:** Article 26(7) is a duty on the employer and it is easy to miss because it sits outside the technical requirements: before a high-risk system is used at the workplace, the affected workers and their representatives have to be told. Record when, and to whom.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 26(2) — Obligations of deployers — assigned human oversight](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/26/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 26(4) — Obligations of deployers — input data](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/26/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 26(7) — Obligations of deployers — informing workers](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/26/)\n_In force from 2027-12-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "human-oversight",
                  "upcoming"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eu-ai-act.art86.right-to-explanation",
              "name": "EuAiActArt86RightToExplanation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Explain an individual decision to the person it was taken about"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 86(1) gives any affected person subject to a decision the deployer takes on the basis of output from an Annex III high-risk system — other than the critical-infrastructure systems in point 2 — which produces legal effects or similarly significantly affects them in a way they consider adverse to their health, safety or fundamental rights, the right to obtain from the deployer clear and meaningful explanations of the role of the AI system in the decision procedure and of the main elements of the decision taken. Article 86(3) applies it only to the extent the right is not already provided for under other Union law."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 86(1) gives any affected person subject to a decision the deployer takes on the basis of output from an Annex III high-risk system — other than the critical-infrastructure systems in point 2 — which produces legal effects or similarly significantly affects them in a way they consider adverse to their health, safety or fundamental rights, the right to obtain from the deployer clear and meaningful explanations of the role of the AI system in the decision procedure and of the main elements of the decision taken. Article 86(3) applies it only to the extent the right is not already provided for under other Union law.\n\nThis right has applied since 2 August 2026, ahead of the Chapter III duties over the same system. Record, with each decision, the role the system played and the main elements of the outcome, and publish how a person asks for them. Where a GDPR Article 22(3) route already exists, Article 86(3) means extending it is enough — but Article 86 asks about the role of the system in the procedure, which Article 22(3) does not.\n\nEU AI Act Art. 86(1) — Right to explanation of individual decision-making: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/86/\nEU AI Act Art. 86(3) — The right applies only where Union law does not already provide it: https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/86/",
                "markdown": "**Explain an individual decision to the person it was taken about**\nArticle 86(1) gives any affected person subject to a decision the deployer takes on the basis of output from an Annex III high-risk system — other than the critical-infrastructure systems in point 2 — which produces legal effects or similarly significantly affects them in a way they consider adverse to their health, safety or fundamental rights, the right to obtain from the deployer clear and meaningful explanations of the role of the AI system in the decision procedure and of the main elements of the decision taken. Article 86(3) applies it only to the extent the right is not already provided for under other Union law.\n**How to close it:** This right has applied since 2 August 2026, ahead of the Chapter III duties over the same system. Record, with each decision, the role the system played and the main elements of the outcome, and publish how a person asks for them. Where a GDPR Article 22(3) route already exists, Article 86(3) means extending it is enough — but Article 86 asks about the role of the system in the procedure, which Article 22(3) does not.\n**Citations**\n- [EU AI Act Art. 86(1) — Right to explanation of individual decision-making](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/86/)\n- [EU AI Act Art. 86(3) — The right applies only where Union law does not already provide it](https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/86/)\n_In force from 2026-08-02._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "eu-ai-act",
                  "rights",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "gdpr.art22.human-intervention",
              "name": "GdprArt22HumanIntervention",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Right to human intervention in an automated decision"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 22(1) gives a data subject the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing which produces legal effects or similarly significantly affects them. Where such processing is permitted, Article 22(3) requires safeguards including at least the right to obtain human intervention, to express a point of view, and to contest the decision."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 22(1) gives a data subject the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing which produces legal effects or similarly significantly affects them. Where such processing is permitted, Article 22(3) requires safeguards including at least the right to obtain human intervention, to express a point of view, and to contest the decision.\n\nAdd a route for the affected person to reach a human who can review and reverse the decision, and record that the human actually considered it.\n\nGDPR Art. 22(1) — Automated individual decision-making, including profiling: https://gdpr-info.eu/\nGDPR Art. 22(3) — Safeguards — human intervention, point of view, contest: https://gdpr-info.eu/",
                "markdown": "**Right to human intervention in an automated decision**\nArticle 22(1) gives a data subject the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing which produces legal effects or similarly significantly affects them. Where such processing is permitted, Article 22(3) requires safeguards including at least the right to obtain human intervention, to express a point of view, and to contest the decision.\n**How to close it:** Add a route for the affected person to reach a human who can review and reverse the decision, and record that the human actually considered it.\n**Citations**\n- [GDPR Art. 22(1) — Automated individual decision-making, including profiling](https://gdpr-info.eu/)\n- [GDPR Art. 22(3) — Safeguards — human intervention, point of view, contest](https://gdpr-info.eu/)\n_In force from 2018-05-25._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "gdpr",
                  "rights",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "gdpr.art13-15.meaningful-information",
              "name": "GdprArt1315MeaningfulInformation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Meaningful information about the logic involved"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Articles 13(2)(f), 14(2)(g) and 15(1)(h) require the controller, where automated decision-making under Article 22 takes place, to provide meaningful information about the logic involved and the significance and envisaged consequences of the processing for the data subject."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Articles 13(2)(f), 14(2)(g) and 15(1)(h) require the controller, where automated decision-making under Article 22 takes place, to provide meaningful information about the logic involved and the significance and envisaged consequences of the processing for the data subject.\n\nProduce reason codes or a short rationale alongside each decision, and store it with the decision so it can be surfaced on a subject access request.\n\nGDPR Art. 13(2)(f) — Information to be provided — logic involved: https://gdpr-info.eu/\nGDPR Art. 15(1)(h) — Right of access — logic involved: https://gdpr-info.eu/",
                "markdown": "**Meaningful information about the logic involved**\nArticles 13(2)(f), 14(2)(g) and 15(1)(h) require the controller, where automated decision-making under Article 22 takes place, to provide meaningful information about the logic involved and the significance and envisaged consequences of the processing for the data subject.\n**How to close it:** Produce reason codes or a short rationale alongside each decision, and store it with the decision so it can be surfaced on a subject access request.\n**Citations**\n- [GDPR Art. 13(2)(f) — Information to be provided — logic involved](https://gdpr-info.eu/)\n- [GDPR Art. 15(1)(h) — Right of access — logic involved](https://gdpr-info.eu/)\n_In force from 2018-05-25._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "gdpr",
                  "transparency",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "gdpr.art35.dpia",
              "name": "GdprArt35Dpia",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Data protection impact assessment"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 35(1) requires a DPIA where processing is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons. Article 35(3)(a) names systematic and extensive evaluation of personal aspects based on automated processing, including profiling, on which decisions producing legal or similarly significant effects are based."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 35(1) requires a DPIA where processing is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons. Article 35(3)(a) names systematic and extensive evaluation of personal aspects based on automated processing, including profiling, on which decisions producing legal or similarly significant effects are based.\n\nCarry out a DPIA before the processing starts. Under AI Act Article 27(4), a fundamental rights impact assessment may cross-reference or incorporate it, so doing this once serves both regimes.\n\nGDPR Art. 35(1) — Data protection impact assessment: https://gdpr-info.eu/\nGDPR Art. 35(3)(a) — When a DPIA is required — automated evaluation of personal aspects: https://gdpr-info.eu/",
                "markdown": "**Data protection impact assessment**\nArticle 35(1) requires a DPIA where processing is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons. Article 35(3)(a) names systematic and extensive evaluation of personal aspects based on automated processing, including profiling, on which decisions producing legal or similarly significant effects are based.\n**How to close it:** Carry out a DPIA before the processing starts. Under AI Act Article 27(4), a fundamental rights impact assessment may cross-reference or incorporate it, so doing this once serves both regimes.\n**Citations**\n- [GDPR Art. 35(1) — Data protection impact assessment](https://gdpr-info.eu/)\n- [GDPR Art. 35(3)(a) — When a DPIA is required — automated evaluation of personal aspects](https://gdpr-info.eu/)\n_In force from 2018-05-25._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "gdpr",
                  "risk-management",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "gdpr.art17.erasure",
              "name": "GdprArt17Erasure",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Erasure and rectification of personal data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Articles 16 and 17 give data subjects the right to rectification of inaccurate personal data and to erasure. For an AI system this reaches the inputs, the stored decisions and any derived features."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Articles 16 and 17 give data subjects the right to rectification of inaccurate personal data and to erasure. For an AI system this reaches the inputs, the stored decisions and any derived features.\n\nImplement deletion and correction that reaches the decision log as well as the primary record. Note the tension with AI Act Article 19: logs must be retained for six months, so erasure normally means pseudonymising the log rather than dropping it.\n\nGDPR Art. 16 — Right to rectification: https://gdpr-info.eu/\nGDPR Art. 17 — Right to erasure: https://gdpr-info.eu/",
                "markdown": "**Erasure and rectification of personal data**\nArticles 16 and 17 give data subjects the right to rectification of inaccurate personal data and to erasure. For an AI system this reaches the inputs, the stored decisions and any derived features.\n**How to close it:** Implement deletion and correction that reaches the decision log as well as the primary record. Note the tension with AI Act Article 19: logs must be retained for six months, so erasure normally means pseudonymising the log rather than dropping it.\n**Citations**\n- [GDPR Art. 16 — Right to rectification](https://gdpr-info.eu/)\n- [GDPR Art. 17 — Right to erasure](https://gdpr-info.eu/)\n_In force from 2018-05-25._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "gdpr",
                  "rights",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "gdpr.art9.special-category",
              "name": "GdprArt9SpecialCategory",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lawful basis for special-category data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Article 9(1) prohibits processing personal data revealing racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious beliefs, trade union membership, genetic or biometric data for unique identification, health data, or data concerning sex life or sexual orientation, unless one of the Article 9(2) conditions applies."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Article 9(1) prohibits processing personal data revealing racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious beliefs, trade union membership, genetic or biometric data for unique identification, health data, or data concerning sex life or sexual orientation, unless one of the Article 9(2) conditions applies.\n\nIdentify the Article 9(2) condition for each attribute before processing. Collecting protected attributes solely to measure bias is defensible, but it needs its own recorded basis.\n\nGDPR Art. 9(1) — Processing of special categories of personal data: https://gdpr-info.eu/\nGDPR Art. 9(2) — Conditions for lawful processing: https://gdpr-info.eu/",
                "markdown": "**Lawful basis for special-category data**\nArticle 9(1) prohibits processing personal data revealing racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious beliefs, trade union membership, genetic or biometric data for unique identification, health data, or data concerning sex life or sexual orientation, unless one of the Article 9(2) conditions applies.\n**How to close it:** Identify the Article 9(2) condition for each attribute before processing. Collecting protected attributes solely to measure bias is defensible, but it needs its own recorded basis.\n**Citations**\n- [GDPR Art. 9(1) — Processing of special categories of personal data](https://gdpr-info.eu/)\n- [GDPR Art. 9(2) — Conditions for lawful processing](https://gdpr-info.eu/)\n_In force from 2018-05-25._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "gdpr",
                  "data-governance",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "nyc-ll144.bias-audit",
              "name": "NycLl144BiasAudit",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Annual independent bias audit"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "6 RCNY § 5-301(a): an employer or employment agency must not use an automated employment decision tool if more than one year has passed since its most recent bias audit, which must be carried out by an independent auditor."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "6 RCNY § 5-301(a): an employer or employment agency must not use an automated employment decision tool if more than one year has passed since its most recent bias audit, which must be carried out by an independent auditor.\n\nCommission an independent bias audit before use. Each day of use without a current audit is a separate violation under § 20-872.\n\nNYC Local Law 144 § 5-301(a) — Bias audit — annual requirement: https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/about/automated-employment-decision-tools.page\nNYC Local Law 144 § 5-300 — Definitions — 'independent auditor': https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/about/automated-employment-decision-tools.page",
                "markdown": "**Annual independent bias audit**\n6 RCNY § 5-301(a): an employer or employment agency must not use an automated employment decision tool if more than one year has passed since its most recent bias audit, which must be carried out by an independent auditor.\n**How to close it:** Commission an independent bias audit before use. Each day of use without a current audit is a separate violation under § 20-872.\n**Citations**\n- [NYC Local Law 144 § 5-301(a) — Bias audit — annual requirement](https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/about/automated-employment-decision-tools.page)\n- [NYC Local Law 144 § 5-300 — Definitions — 'independent auditor'](https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/about/automated-employment-decision-tools.page)\n_In force from 2023-07-05._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "compliance",
                  "nyc-ll144",
                  "data-governance",
                  "in-force"
                ],
                "security-severity": "9.5",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "nyc-ll144.impact-ratio",
              "name": "NycLl144ImpactRatio",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Selection rate and impact ratio by category"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "6 RCNY § 5-301(b)-(c): the audit must calculate the selection rate (or scoring rate) and the impact ratio for each category, separately for sex categories, race/ethnicity categories, and intersectional sex × ethnicity × race categories, using EEO-1 Component 1 categories."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "6 RCNY § 5-301(b)-(c): the audit must calculate the selection rate (or scoring rate) and the impact ratio for each category, separately for sex categories, race/ethnicity categories, and intersectional sex × ethnicity × race categories, using EEO-1 Component 1 categories.\n\nImplement: impact ratio = selection rate for a category ÷ selection rate of the most selected category, computed for sex, race/ethnicity and intersectional categories, plus a count of individuals in an unknown category.\n\nNYC Local Law 144 § 5-301(b) — Bias audit — selection-type AEDT: https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/about/automated-employment-decision-tools.page\nNYC Local Law 144 § 5-301(c) — Bias audit — scoring-type AEDT: https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/about/automated-employment-decision-tools.page\nNYC Local Law 144 § 5-300 — Impact ratio formula: https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/about/automated-employment-decision-tools.page",
                "markdown": "**Selection rate and impact ratio by category**\n6 RCNY § 5-301(b)-(c): the audit must calculate the selection rate (or scoring rate) and the impact ratio for each category, separately for sex categories, race/ethnicity categories, and intersectional sex × ethnicity × race categories, using EEO-1 Component 1 categories.\n**How to close it:** Implement: impact ratio = selection rate for a category ÷ selection rate of the most selected category, computed for sex, race/ethnicity and intersectional categories, plus a count of individuals in an unknown category.\n**Citations**\n- [NYC Local Law 144 § 5-301(b) — Bias audit — selection-type AEDT](https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/about/automated-employment-decision-tools.page)\n- [NYC Local Law 144 § 5-301(c) — Bias audit — scoring-type AEDT](https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/about/automated-employment-decision-tools.page)\n- [NYC Local Law 144 § 5-300 — Impact ratio formula](https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/about/automated-employment-decision-tools.page)\n_In force from 2023-07-05._"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
