                  "upcoming"
                ],
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "precision": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "colorado-admt.developer-documentation",
              "name": "ColoradoAdmtDeveloperDocumentation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Documentation handed to deployers"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A developer must give each deployer technical documentation stating the intended uses and known harmful or inappropriate uses, the categories of training data, known limitations and risks, instructions for appropriate use, monitoring and meaningful human review, and the information the deployer needs for its own consumer disclosures. Records must be retained for at least three years."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A developer must give each deployer technical documentation stating the intended uses and known harmful or inappropriate uses, the categories of training data, known limitations and risks, instructions for appropriate use, monitoring and meaningful human review, and the information the deployer needs for its own consumer disclosures. Records must be retained for at least three years.\n\nWrite it once against the Colorado list; it also answers AI Act Article 13(3), so the work is shared across both regimes.\n\nColorado ADMT Act § 6-1-1702 — Developer duties — documentation to deployers: https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/sb26-189",
                "markdown": "**Documentation handed to deployers**\nA developer must give each deployer technical documentation stating the intended uses and known harmful or inappropriate uses, the categories of training data, known limitations and risks, instructions for appropriate use, monitoring and meaningful human review, and the information the deployer needs for its own consumer disclosures. Records must be retained for at least three years.\n**How to close it:** Write it once against the Colorado list; it also answers AI Act Article 13(3), so the work is shared across both regimes.\n**Citations**\n- [Colorado ADMT Act § 6-1-1702 — Developer duties — documentation to deployers](https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/sb26-189)\n_In force from 2027-01-01._"
              },
